// mgeHost64 — WHAT UNITS DOES THE SCENE TARGET WANT, AND HOW DOES A PASS GET INTO THEM.
// tasks/forge-postprocess.md step 6a.
//
// Until step 6a every colour frag ended `c = tonemap(c)`, so pMSAAColor held DISPLAY-REFERRED values
// whatever its format — which is why the fp16 probe looked identical and why bloom had nothing to
// bloom from. The curve now runs ONCE, at the very end, inside resolve.frag, and the scene target
// carries radiance.
//
// ONE lane decides: gShadowParams.toneParams.x, the host's folded `g_hdrSceneColor && sampleCount>1`
// (shadowparams.h.fsl). A shader must never re-derive it from a format or a sample count — the two
// halves of that condition have to move together, and they stay together because only the host ever
// evaluates them.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE RULE. Every pass that writes to the scene target falls in exactly one of three classes, and
// THIS LIST IS THE CLASSIFICATION — not a plan document, not a commit message. Step 6a shipped once
// with sky.frag in the wrong class, and the whole cost of that was that the rule lived somewhere a
// shader author would never look.
//
//   1. LIT / GENERATED, and it already called tonemap() before 6a   ->  tonemapInPass()
//      opaque, statics, terrain, alpha, multimap, multimap_alpha, water's sun specular.
//      The curve moves out of the pass; the pass writes radiance.
//
//   2. MW's AUTHORED DISPLAY-REFERRED DATA, and it never called tonemap()  ->  liftInPass()
//      sky.frag (MW's own per-vertex atmosphere colours x MW's sky/moon/star/cloud textures),
//      statics_add.frag (MW's glow map), statics_blend.frag (hero blend layers).
//      These reached the target UNCURVED, so moving the curve to the end would apply it to them for
//      the FIRST time and darken them — up to 41/255 at the top end (tonemap(1.0) = 0.84). Lifting
//      them through the inverse means the final curve hands the authored value back untouched.
//
//      ⚠⚠ **AMENDED 2026-08-15 (step 3 / S2): CLASS 2 IS NOW INERT, AND THE THREE PASSES ABOVE ARE
//      EFFECTIVELY CLASS 3.** liftInPass() returns its argument unchanged whenever the AgX curve is
//      armed. The lift was never "the sky is a reference" as a principle — it was the INVERSE OF ONE
//      PARTICULAR CURVE, the semi-HDR cubic in tonemap.h.fsl, and AgX is a different curve. Running
//      inverseTonemap() into AgX does not preserve anything; it just pre-distorts the sky by the
//      inverse of a function nothing applies any more. The class survives as a CLASSIFICATION (these
//      three passes still reproduce MW's authored data, and if a closed-form AgX inverse ever exists
//      this is where it goes) but the operation is gone, and the fog/interior/underwater NEUTRALITY
//      CHECK that used to gate it is expected to FAIL under AgX. Do not chase it — it was the test
//      that the lift worked, and the lift is gone by decision (forge-postprocess.md, S2).
//
//      The exposure servo had already broken the round trip a step earlier
//      (tonemap(E·inverseTonemap(a)) != a for any E != 1) and the decision there was the same one:
//      the sky JOINS the exposed world. This finishes it.
//
//   3. GENUINELY GENERATED RADIANCE WITH ITS OWN INTENSITY KNOB  ->  neither; write it raw
//      glow.frag (Colour x Intensity x f^2). It is not reproducing anything authored, it WANTS to
//      exceed 1.0 once there is a container for it, and liftInPass would clamp exactly that away.
//      If it reads dim under HDR, the answer is its Intensity knob, not a lift.
//
//   ...plus one thing that is in NO class: statics_multiply.frag writes a MULTIPLIER (DST_COLOR/ZERO
//   dark map), not a colour. A scale factor has no units to convert, so it is left alone — but note
//   that a multiply now lands in scene-referred space, which is a real (and more correct) change.
//
// HOW TO CLASSIFY A NEW PASS, in one question: **does this pass reproduce a value MW authored, or
// does it make one up?** Reproduced -> lift it, and the image cannot move. Made up -> it is radiance;
// let the resolve roll it off.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ⚠ NEITHER FUNCTION DOES ANYTHING IN THE LDR BUILD. Display-referred, tonemapInPass IS tonemap()
// and liftInPass is the identity — i.e. every pass keeps exactly the behaviour it had before 6a, so
// `g_hdrSceneColor = false` stays byte-identical to the pre-6a image. That is the A/B, and it is
// also why a class-2 pass gets liftInPass() rather than "just add the tonemap it was missing": the
// two are the same under HDR but only one of them leaves LDR alone.
//
// Requires (include AFTER opaque.srt.h, AFTER tonemap.h.fsl and AFTER linearize.h.fsl):
// gShadowParams + tonemap()/inverseTonemap() + srgbToLinear()/linearToSrgb()/mod2xLinear(). Both
// maths files deliberately stay free of any SRT dependency, because resolve.frag includes them and
// cannot see gShadowParams — the maths and the policy are separate files precisely so the resolve
// can have one without the other. The includes are listed per shader rather than nested here, which
// is the house convention for .h.fsl and keeps the order visible at each call site.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// STEP 5 — THE SECOND AXIS: WHAT DOMAIN ARE THOSE UNITS IN.
//
// 6a answered "how bright" (display-referred vs scene-referred). It did not answer "in what
// encoding", and the answer was: MW's, i.e. gamma. Step 5 migrates the domain, and the classification
// above is EXACTLY the right place for it, because the question it already asks — *does this pass
// reproduce a value MW authored, or does it make one up?* — is the same question the decode asks.
// Reproduced => it arrived gamma-encoded and must be decoded. Made up => it is already radiance.
//
// ONE MORE LANE DECIDES: gShadowParams.toneParams.y, the host's `g_linearScene && sceneReferred`.
// Same shape, same rule, same reason as toneParams.x — a shader must never re-derive it, and linear
// rides ON TOP of scene-referred because 8-bit linear bands in the darks and a UNORM target cannot
// hold what a linear sum overshoots.
//
// WHERE THE DECODE ACTUALLY HAPPENS, and it is mostly NOT here:
//
//   TEXTURES   — hardware, via _SRGB views (forgerender.cpp's DDS format map). NEVER a shader pow():
//                the hardware decodes BEFORE filtering and a pow() cannot. See linearize.h.fsl.
//   CBUFFERS   — host-side, at the single write site (sun/ambient/fog/zenith/light colours). The
//                fogColNear lift is the established idiom and now composes with it.
//   VERTEX +
//   PER-DRAW   — decodeAuthored() in the VERTEX stage. Decode-then-interpolate is the same ordering
//                rule the hardware follows for texels; decoding in the frag would interpolate in
//                gamma and linearise after, the exact mistake the _SRGB view exists to prevent.
//
// THE ALGEBRA, because it decides what may move. Multiplication commutes with the transfer function
// and addition does not, so every MODULATING stage is preserved and every SUM is where the migration
// lives. MOD is exact; MOD2X needs mod2xLinear() (linearize.h.fsl) and is the one op with a code
// change; ADD moves. The biggest sum in the renderer is `ambient + sun·N·L`, and it moves the most:
// a 0.4/0.6 pair that summed to 1.0 in gamma sums to 0.458 in linear, ~0.70 once re-encoded. Lit
// surfaces where two light terms meet therefore DARKEN, and that is not a regression — it is the
// gamma-space over-brightening that abot's Darkening mod has been cancelling by hand all along
// (its caveLightnessPerc 42 lands inside the same numbers). Dropping abot and going linear is
// close to a wash, which is why the plan makes them two separately-measured steps.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#ifndef SCENE_COLOR_H
#define SCENE_COLOR_H

// Class 1. The curve, still in the pass, until the scene goes scene-referred.
float3 tonemapInPass(float3 c)
{
    return (gShadowParams.toneParams.x > 0.5f) ? c : tonemap(c);
}

// Class 2. The mirror image: nothing to do while the target is display-referred, and a lift into
// scene-referred units when it is not, so resolve.frag's curve returns the authored value.
//
// The [0,1] clamp inside inverseTonemap() is CORRECT here rather than merely safe: the target this
// reproduces was B8G8R8A8_UNORM, which clipped at 1.0, so anything a class-2 pass pushed above 1
// was never on screen. Lifting the CLIPPED value reproduces what was actually displayed.
float3 liftInPass(float3 c)
{
    if (gShadowParams.toneParams.x <= 0.5f) { return c; }
    // STEP 3 / S2 — THE LIFT IS DEAD UNDER AgX. See the class-2 amendment at the top of this file:
    // everything below inverts tonemap.h.fsl's cubic specifically, and under AgX nothing applies
    // that cubic. toneParams.w is the host's folded curve select, the SAME expression resolve.frag
    // receives as gResolveParams.tone.y — one host expression, two receivers, so the pass that
    // applies the curve and the passes that pre-compensate for it can never disagree about WHICH
    // curve. (That lane's previous tenant, the air-fog changeover depth, is documented as retired in
    // shadowparams.h.fsl.)
    if (gShadowParams.toneParams.w > 0.5f) { return c; }
    // Step 5. The lift's CONTRACT is unchanged — "make the end of the frame hand this authored value
    // back untouched" — but the end of the frame now runs tonemap(linearToSrgb(x)), so the value that
    // has to reach it through the encode is inverseTonemap(authored), and the authored value must
    // first be recovered out of the linear domain this pass is already working in (the texel came
    // through an _SRGB view; the vertex colour through decodeAuthored). Hence the round trip: encode,
    // lift, decode. Composition, not a correction — each half is exactly the transform it says.
    if (gShadowParams.toneParams.y > 0.5f) {
        return srgbToLinear(inverseTonemap(linearToSrgb(c)));
    }
    return inverseTonemap(c);
}

// ─── Step 5: the domain ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Is the scene target LINEAR? For a pass that needs to branch on it structurally. Note this is a
// cbuffer bit, i.e. wave-uniform but NOT compile-time — DXC will not strip what it guards
// ([[project_uniform_branch_is_not_free]]), so guard whole blocks, never single instructions.
bool sceneIsLinear()
{
    return gShadowParams.toneParams.y > 0.5f;
}

// MW authored this colour for a gamma display. Put it in the scene's domain.
//
// VERTEX STAGE ONLY for anything interpolated (vertex colours, and the per-draw material lanes that
// ride the instance stream beside them). Decode-then-interpolate; never the reverse.
float3 decodeAuthored(float3 c)
{
    return (gShadowParams.toneParams.y > 0.5f) ? srgbToLinear(c) : c;
}

// Scalar twin — MW's per-vertex emissive STRENGTH (distant statics pack it in Normal.w and add it as
// a grey colour), which is authored on the same display scale as a colour channel and converts the
// same way. ALPHA NEVER COMES HERE: coverage is not a colour and has no transfer function.
float decodeAuthored1(float c)
{
    return (gShadowParams.toneParams.y > 0.5f) ? srgbToLinear1(c) : c;
}

// MW's MODULATE2X stage, in whichever domain the scene is in. The literal 2 is a display-space
// number — see mod2xLinear() in linearize.h.fsl for why a constant cannot replace it under the
// piecewise curve. `t` is the SAMPLED texel, i.e. already decoded by the _SRGB view when linear.
float3 mod2xStage(float3 t)
{
    return (gShadowParams.toneParams.y > 0.5f) ? mod2xLinear(t) : (t * 2.0f);
}

// ─── AN SDR-EXPOSED EMITTER IS NOT A RADIANCE MAP ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// MW's emissive textures are ALREADY-EXPOSED images. A flame texture is what a flame LOOKS like once
// an eye or a camera has compressed it: white-hot core, soft glowing skirt. That skirt is not "less
// flame" — it is enormous radiance seen through a compressive curve. srgbToLinear above inverts the
// TRANSFER FUNCTION; nothing inverts the TONE CURVE. So multiplying such a texture by a large
// emissive gain re-expands core and skirt by the SAME factor, and the ratio between them — which is
// the entire shape of the flame — is destroyed.
//
// MEASURED, on the mod flame that exposed this (textures\r0\r0_candleflame.dds): 78.9% of texels sit
// above LUMA 0.9, mean luma 0.893 in the faint skirt against 0.893 in the hot core, correlation with
// alpha 0.14. At gain 387 every texel with a > 1/387 clips to white — 68.8% of that texture — and a
// soft billboard renders as a SOLID ROUNDED SQUARE.
//
// ⚠ LUMA WAS THE WRONG INSTRUMENT AND THOSE NUMBERS LED SOMEWHERE WRONG. A flat luma profile is not a
// flat IMAGE: luma is dominated by R and G, so a saturated orange (1.0, 0.6, 0.2) and a pure white
// score almost the same and the flame reads as featureless. It is not — it has a white core, an
// orange body and a red skirt, and that CHROMA structure is the exposure information. The first
// version of this operator therefore drove itself from luma * coverage, which failed twice over: it
// could not tell core from body at all, and folding coverage in faded the emissive faster than the
// alpha blending it and ringed every silhouette.
//
// Its sibling r0_candleflame_blu.dds never showed the square, which is what made this look like a
// per-asset mystery: two thirds of it is fully transparent, so far less of it was in a position to
// clip. Same mod, same CandleFlameAnimNode, opposite outcomes from how the two were authored.
//
// So the gain must not land uniformly. ONLY THE TEXELS THE ENCODE CLIPPED LOST HEADROOM, AND ONLY
// THOSE OWE RADIANCE BACK. The coloured parts were never clipped; they already ARE the exposed look
// and must keep it.
//
// ⚠⚠ AND CLIPPING IS PER-CHANNEL, NOT A PROPERTY OF THE TEXEL. Getting this wrong is what put BLACK
// SPECKLES inside the flame core. The first attempt used a scalar whiteness, w = min(r,g,b), on the
// reasoning that a blown-out highlight is achromatic. True of the core, but the flame body is
// SATURATED ORANGE WITH ITS RED CHANNEL ALREADY CLIPPED — measured in the frame at (1.084, 0.270,
// 0.010) — and min() calls that 0.01, i.e. "not clipped, leave it exposed". It then sat at 1x
// beside neighbours at 216x and read as a hole. (Measured, not inferred: zero non-finite values in
// that dump and exactly ONE negative pixel in 3.1M, so neither NaN nor a sign error was involved.)
// The opposite scalar, max(r,g,b), fails the other way — the body's red is clipped almost everywhere,
// so it boosts nearly the whole quad and the above-white fraction goes back from 24.5% to 86.6%,
// which is the square returning.
//
// Per channel, all three regions fall out of ONE rule with no special cases:
//
//     f_c = fMin + (1 - fMin) * pow(saturate(albedo_c), p)
//
//   white core   (1.00, 1.00, 1.00)  -> f = (1, 1, 1)          full gain, stays white-hot
//   orange body  (1.00, 0.32, 0.03)  -> f = (1, ~0.001, ~0)    red recovers, G/B do not: BRIGHT RED
//   dim skirt    (0.30, 0.10, 0.02)  -> f = (~0, ~0, ~0)       keeps its exposed look
//
// The body is no longer a hole because its clipped channel is restored, and the hue it lands on —
// red — is exactly what a flame texel whose red ran out of range physically is.
//
// It is far sharper in LINEAR than the sRGB numbers suggest, which is what makes a modest exponent
// enough: an sRGB orange (1.0, 0.6, 0.2) decodes to (1.0, 0.318, 0.033).
//
// fMin IS SELF-NORMALISING, and that is the part a fixed knee could not do. The skirt has to come out
// at "the exposed look", i.e. unit emissive — so the floor is 1/luma(emis), which lands emis * fMin
// back at magnitude 1 whatever the gain happens to be. A fixed floor cannot: to bring a gain of 245
// down to 1 needs 0.004, while the same constant applied to a gain of 3 would extinguish it.
//
// p is the falloff sharpness (gShadowParams.skyAO2.w; 0 = exact bypass).
//
// ⚠ COVERAGE IS DELIBERATELY NOT AN INPUT, AND WAS, AND THAT WAS A BUG. Folding alpha into the
// measure made the emissive fall off FASTER than the coverage that blends it, so the emissive-lit
// core faded into the diffuse-lit skirt at a different rate and left a visible RING at the
// silhouette. Whiteness comes from the texture alone, so it is stable across the silhouette and the
// alpha blend is left to do the fading it was already doing correctly.
//
// ⚠ BRANCH on p, never scale by it. 0 must be an exact bypass rather than `emis * 1.0`, because a NaN
// upstream survives a multiply by one ([[project_nan_survives_zero_multiply]]).
//
// ⚠ SAFE ON NON-EMISSIVE DRAWS with no gate: emis = 0 gives luma 0, max(luma, 1) = 1, fMin = 1, so
// f = 1 identically and nothing moves. The operator can only ever pull emissive DOWN toward its
// authored value — it never adds energy, and it never touches a surface that has none.
// ⚠ THE MEASURE IS THE PREMULTIPLIED VALUE, NOT THE TEXTURE COLOUR. Two revisions of this operator
// read albedo.rgb alone and both produced a bright SQUARE where the flame should be a teardrop.
// R0_candleflame.dds (512x1024, DXT5) says why: max(RGB) >= 0.99 across 97% of the texels its ALPHA
// calls transparent — the RGB plane is a flat bright field and the SHAPE IS ENTIRELY IN ALPHA
// (mean 0.204; 52% of texels below a = 0.02). MW's particle-flame textures are authored that way as
// a family. A measure taken from rgb is therefore ~1 over the whole quad, hands the full gain
// (k_eff 402 for this fixture) to the transparent corners, and a 0.005 alpha times 402 is opaque.
//
// Multiplying by coverage inside the saturate fixes it AND is the physically honest form: `rgb * a`
// is what MW displayed, so `rgb * a` is what the 8-bit encode clipped.
//
// It must go INSIDE the measure, never onto the result. An earlier revision multiplied the delta by
// coverage and ringed the silhouette: f already bottoms out at fMin = 1/lumaE (the "hand the
// authored exposed value back" floor, which is BELOW 1), so scaling by coverage on top of that
// drove the near-edge emissive below MW's own value and drew a dark band just outside the core.
//
// SCALAR, not per channel. A per-channel factor recovers only the channel that clipped and divides
// the others by lumaE, which turns the flame's orange body into a near-black deep red — visible as
// dark speckles among the white-hot core. Whichever channel ran out of range, the TEXEL hit the
// ceiling, so the whole texel is scaled by one number and the hue is preserved.
float3 expandExposedEmissive(float3 emis, float3 albedoRgb, float cov)
{
    float p = gShadowParams.skyAO2.w;
    if (!(p > 0.0f)) { return emis; }
    // The measure is the EXPOSED value — albedo x coverage — because that product is what MW put on
    // screen and therefore what the 8-bit encode clipped. max() over the channels, because a
    // highlight runs out of range in whichever channel is largest; and SCALAR, applied to all three
    // emissive channels alike, so the texel keeps its hue. saturate() matters: albedo can exceed 1
    // once the dev-panel albedo scale is applied, and pow() past 1 would AMPLIFY, not attenuate.
    float3 a = max(albedoRgb, float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f));
    float  w = saturate(max(max(a.r, a.g), a.b) * max(cov, 0.0f));
    float  lumaE = dot(max(emis, float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f)), float3(0.2126f, 0.7152f, 0.0722f));
    float  fMin  = 1.0f / max(lumaE, 1.0f);
    float  f = fMin + (1.0f - fMin) * pow(w, p);
    return emis * f;
}

// The same operator as the DELTA to add to an ALREADY-ASSEMBLED `lit`. Every colour frag folds emis
// into lit before it has sampled its albedo, and `lit` is read by debug views in between — so this
// lets the correction land at the composite (`c = albedo.rgb * lit`) without relocating the material
// routing or duplicating a texture fetch. Adding `emis * (f - 1)` is identically replacing `emis`
// with `emis * f`.
float3 expandExposedEmissiveDelta(float3 emis, float3 albedoRgb, float cov)
{
    return expandExposedEmissive(emis, albedoRgb, cov) - emis;
}

#endif // SCENE_COLOR_H
